/* A StackOverflowError that reaches the top of a *thread*: the report names the
 * thread, the thread ends, and the process goes on -- which is Java's rule for
 * an uncaught throwable on a thread that is not the last one (ThreadGroup's
 * uncaughtException prints the line; the thread then dies and main carries on).
 *
 * It is the other half of t215: there the report ends the process with a status,
 * here it ends one thread, and the same margin has to hold the report either
 * way. The thread's own stack is what overflowed, so a runtime that reported it
 * on the faulting stack would have nothing to report it with.
 *
 * The name in the line is the one the Thread was constructed with. Java prints
 * `Exception in thread "worker" java.lang.StackOverflowError` and then the two
 * lines from main, exit 0. */

class Main {
  static int frameRead(int n) {
    if (n == 0) {
      return 0
    }
    int[] frame = new int[4]
    frame[0] = n
    int r = frameRead(n - 1)
    return r + frame[0] - n + 1
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    Thread worker = new Thread(new Runnable() {
      public void run() {
        System.out.println(frameRead(10000000))
      }
    }, "worker")
    worker.start()
    worker.join()
    System.out.println("thread alive: " + worker.isAlive())
    System.out.println("main still running")
  }
}
